Currently requires a `tryCatch()`:

```r
vec_is_coercible <- function(x, to, ..., x_arg = "x", to_arg = "to") {
  tryCatch(
    vctrs_error_incompatible_type = function(...) FALSE,
    {
      vctrs::vec_ptype2(x, to, ..., x_arg = x_arg, y_arg = to_arg)
      TRUE
    }
  )
}
```

Do we want to change `vec_ptype2()` to communicate non-coercibility via a sentinel rather than an error? `tryCatch()` is currently slow and causes complex backtraces, but that might get better in future versions of R, depending on https://github.com/lionel-/r-source/pull/22 being accepted (local handlers probably won't be exposed to users, but `tryCatch()` might be rewritten to use them).

Need to benchmark the two strategies: in the first we just put in a list and then `vec_c()` at the end; in the second we assume that the type is mostly stable so we can create the space for output in the beginning, and only occasionally change.

```R
vec_map <- function(.x, .f, ..., .ptype = NULL) {
  .x <- lapply(.x, .f, ...)
  vec_c(!!!.x, .ptype = .ptype)
}


vec_map <- function(.x, .f, ..., .ptype = NULL) {
  first <- .x[[1]]
  first_out <- .f(.x, ...) 
  
  out <- vec_na(first_out, vec_size(.x)) 
  vec_slice(out, 1) <- first_out

  for (i in seq2(2, vec_size(.x))) {
    outi <- .f(vec_slice(x, i), ...)
    if (vec_is(outi, out)) {
      # change type
    }
    vec_slice(out, i) <- outi
  }
  
  out
}
```

