One of the most common uses I have for `vec_assert()` is to do type checking on function arguments. Would it be possible to add an `allow_null` argument?

A very common default for arguments is `NULL`, and I find myself using this pattern quite frequently:

```r
foo <- function(x = NULL) {
  if (!is.null(x)) {
    vec_assert(x, character())
  }
  x
}
```

https://github.com/r-lib/vctrs/blob/81e32472a575a212539bdc9f6b8447e429635f29/src/utils.h#L409

The second check here should be:

```c
(y < 0 && x > (INTMAX_MAX + y))) { 
            ^ right here
```

Which would align with the C CERT standards recommendations
https://wiki.sei.cmu.edu/confluence/display/c/INT32-C.+Ensure+that+operations+on+signed+integers+do+not+result+in+overflow

```c
#include <limits.h>
  
void func(signed int si_a, signed int si_b) {
  signed int diff;
  if ((si_b > 0 && si_a < INT_MIN + si_b) ||
      (si_b < 0 && si_a > INT_MAX + si_b)) {
    /* Handle error */
  } else {
    diff = si_a - si_b;
  }
 
  /* ... */
}
```

The edge case `times = 1` could be made much more efficient by returning early. Maybe also `times = 0` could benefit a little from an early return. This creates nice speed ups e.g. in `tidyr::expand_grid()`.

